      "notes": "All claims are well-grounded. Tier assignments are correct: E7 and E22/E23 are treated as measurable (documented treaty text and regulatory facts); E8 and E34 are correctly projected. The non-regression clause quote (E7) entails the measurable claim. The environmental divergence figures (E22, E23) from brexitfactbase.com are used as supporting context only, not as sole basis for verdict. The direction 'worsens' is justified by the projected removal of the environmental floor, not by party identity. The rationale avoids motive-attribution and is symmetric in its treatment of evidence. Magnitude 'minor' and low confidence are appropriate given the soft policy verb ('prepare for renegotiations') and uncertain renegotiation outcome. The time_split correctly notes near-term negligible effect. No lever_leakage: the verdict judges effect on environmental standards (O6), not trade policy per se. No false_balance concern \u2014 the verdict does not artificially balance; it leans worsens while acknowledging uncertainty about whether renegotiation succeeds. One minor note: E34 is sourced from export.org.uk (advocacy/trade body) \u2014 it is not the sole basis for any claim and is used only to support the projected EU resistance claim, which is also grounded in E33 (Commons Library/European Commission). This is acceptable."
    },
